####  False Color Map analysis

The map above depicts false color imagery for the Eaton and Palisades Fires that took place in January 2025. Landsat raster data was mapped using the shortwave infrared, near-infrared, and red bands to create a false color imagery map. The red on the map despicts fire scars caused by the Eaton and Palisades Fires as the labels show. The green in the map above shows the areas without burn scars while bright green depicts vegetation. Bodies of water including the Pacific Ocean are shown in black in this false imagery. False imagery is being used to more dramatically show the effects of fire on the landscape. 


# Social dimensions of the Eaton and Palisades Fires

Let's explore some of the Environmental Justice Index data. The dataframe includes a variety of environmental justice indices. The aspect of greatest interest to me is the percentile rank of the probability of contracting cancer over the course of a lifetime, assuming continuous exposure to toxic air particulate matters including exposure to PM2.5, ozone, and diesel particulate matter.

The data frame denotes this column as: 

- EPL_TOTCR : Percentile rank of the probability of contracting cancer over the course of a lifetime, assuming continuous exposure.

### Import Environmental Justice Idex (EJI) data

::: {#4a8dccab .cell execution_count=12}
``` {.python .cell-code}
fp_eji = os.path.join('data', 'EJI_2024_California', 'EJI_2024_California.gdb')
eji = gpd.read_file(fp_eji)
```
:::


### Data wrangling

Again, all of the CRS must match to accurately map the intended regions. Since we have already confirmed that the Eaton and Palisades Fires, we can just use the CRS of one of the fire perimeters to change the CRS of our Environmental Justice data. Then we can make sure that the CRS are the same using an assert statement. 

::: {#4624f45e .cell execution_count=13}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Change CRS of eji
eji = eji.to_crs(palisades_fire.crs)

# Make sure CRS are the same 
assert eji.crs == palisades_fire.crs
assert eji.crs == eaton_fire.crs
```
:::


We join the fire perimeter data with the EJI data leaving the default `intersects` parameter. The EJI data is cropped to areas where the  intersection overlaps with the fire perimeters. We'll save these as their own objects to use for mapping below. 

::: {#8a355f41 .cell execution_count=14}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Join the Palisades perimeter and eji data and make sure CRS match
pali_eji = gpd.sjoin(eji, palisades_fire)

# Join the Eaton perimeter and eji data and make sure CRS match
eaton_eji = gpd.sjoin(eji, eaton_fire)
```
:::


Let's look at how the fire perimeters overlap with the census tracts. 

